<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">
<span id="id1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><span id="id1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Millicent Li<sup id="id1.1.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></sup></span><span id="id1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">, <span id="id1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text">Alberto Mario Ceballos Arroyo<sup id="id1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></sup></span>, <span id="id1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text">Giordano Rogers<sup id="id1.1.2.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></sup></span>,
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id1.1.2.3" class="ltx_text">Naomi Saphra<sup id="id1.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">2</span></sup></span>, <span id="id1.1.2.4" class="ltx_text">Byron C. Wallace<sup id="id1.1.2.4.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="id1.1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">1</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Correspondence to li.mil@northeastern.edu.</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
<span id="id3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:397.5pt;">
<span id="id3.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><sup id="id3.1.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Northeastern University,
<sup id="id3.1.2" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Kempner Institute, Harvard University</span>
</span>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Recent interpretability methods have proposed to translate LLM internal representations into natural language descriptions using a second <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verbalizer</span> LLM.
This is intended to illuminate how the target model represents and operates on inputs.
But do such <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">activation verbalization</span> approaches actually provide <em id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">privileged</em> knowledge about the internal workings of the target model, or do they merely convey information about its inputs?
We critically evaluate popular verbalization methods across datasets used in prior work and find that they succeed at benchmarks without any access to target model internals, suggesting that these datasets are not ideal for evaluating verbalization methods.
We then run controlled experiments which reveal that verbalizations often reflect the parametric knowledge of the verbalizer LLM which generated them, rather than the activations of the target LLM being decoded.
Taken together, our results indicate a need for targeted benchmarks and experimental controls to rigorously assess whether verbalization methods provide meaningful insights into the operations of LLMs.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
        Code will be available at <a href="https://www.github.com" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">www.github.com</a> once the paper is finalized.</span></span></span></p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM representations are opaque.
Can we understand them by translating them to natural language?
This sort of <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verbalization</span>—decoding activations into natural language—has been a recent focus in interpretability research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Verbalization uses a second LLM as a <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">verbalizer</span> to translate the activations of the first LLM—the <span id="S1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">target model</span>—into a natural language description.
This approach has been touted as a potential tool to understand “an LLM’s computation” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, allowing one to answer “open-ended questions about model activations” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent work has investigated verbalization techniques for characterizing the inner workings of LLMs <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and identifying harmful knowledge they encode <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Such techniques are exciting because verbalization promises to intuitively communicate (in natural language) <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">privileged</span> knowledge into otherwise opaque model behavior.
Knowledge is considered privileged—as defined in cognitive science <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> and philosophy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alston, 1971</a>)</cite>—if it is only accessible by inspecting internal states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Alston, 1971</a>)</cite>, like model internals, and not via prompting.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, the verbalizer may either communicate privileged information about the target model or may only communicate information readily available in the target model’s <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">input</span> text.
In the latter case, verbalization is of questionable utility from an interpretability perspective; we already have access to the input.
Further complicating things, verbalizers are LLMs with their own implicit world knowledge.
They may draw on this background when decoding target model activations, making it unclear whether generated descriptions reflect the knowledge from the target or the verbalizer model.
Put another way, the descriptions generated from its activations may not be <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faithful</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi &amp; Goldberg, 2020</a>)</cite> to the target model.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13316v1/privileged_info.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:132/36;" width="132" height="36" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Two ways that a verbalizer (<math id="S1.F1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) might describe an activation. In our preferred scenario (a), the description employs privileged information beyond what is accessible in the input (<math id="S1.F1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\textrm{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\textrm{input}}</annotation></semantics></math>), so the country of origin for Alice can be determined from the target (<math id="S1.F1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>) model’s background knowledge.
Alternatively, (b) verbalization may simply reflect the input, providing no further insights into the operations of the <math id="S1.F1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, and so it is impossible to determine where Alice is from.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">We offer evidence that existing verbalization techniques may yield unfaithful descriptions using two tasks:
feature extraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and factual recall <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Meng et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allen-Zhu &amp; Li, 2024</a>)</cite>. First, we establish that benchmarks previously used to evaluate verbalizers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> do not require the verbalizer to use privileged information. Instead, the verbalizer can perform well on these tasks <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without any access to target model internals at all</em> when both the target and verbalizer models have similar knowledge.
We then design a controlled task to test verbalizations for privileged information, finding that they often reflect the verbalizer’s background knowledge rather than the target model’s activations. In summary:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we show that some existing verbalization evaluations cannot support conclusions about target model internals because verbalizer LLMs correctly answer prompts using only input text—without access to activations. These evaluations therefore can only diagnose whether information about the input is <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">removed</span> during processing and not whether the target model <span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">adds</span> its world knowledge to the response.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we find that the verbalizer LLM can implicitly <em id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">invert</em> target model activations to recover input prompts.
Because the input can be reconstructed and sufficiently answered without verbalization, these tasks show that information about the input is <span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">not</span> usually removed from the activations—invalidating the use of some existing benchmarks for verbalization, like feature extraction in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We create a new evaluation task in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> to study whether verbalizers express knowledge <span id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">added</span> by the target model during processing. This setting reveals that verbalizers only describe background knowledge in the weights of the verbalizer, not the target LLM.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, we find that existing evaluations fail to explain the source of knowledge for verbalization. Furthermore, targeted evaluations challenge the faithfulness of current verbalization methods. Future efforts should carefully curate evaluations that clearly articulate the the type of information that the method is intended to provide and design controlled benchmarks accordingly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider several established approaches to verbalization, which we summarize in Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ Does ℳ_2 need to be trained? ‣ 2 Preliminaries ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notation.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Verbalization requires two models: a target LLM <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with layers <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> and a verbalizer LLM <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with layers <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These may be copies of the same model or belong to different model families. Given an input <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}(x_{\text{input}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}(x_{\text{input}})</annotation></semantics></math> yields activations <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> extracted at layer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i^{\text{th}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>i</mi><mtext>th</mtext></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i^{\text{th}}</annotation></semantics></math> token. We want to use <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to decode <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into natural language that reflects the internal states of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, as in <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SelfIE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, both of which <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">patch</span> <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into a specified layer during the inference pass of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Latent Interpretation Tuning, or LIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, instead inserts the concatenated activations from <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">all</span> token positions at a specific layer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into the inference pass of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
When the verbalization methods are paired with an interpretation prompt <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can then be used to decode their input activations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Does <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> need to be trained?</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In general, the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> approach—which subsumes LIT and other probing methods such as <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">logitlens</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">nostalgebraist, 2020</a>)</cite>, <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">FutureLens</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pal et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TunedLens</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belrose et al., 2023</a>)</cite>—assumes that if <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, then no additional finetuning is required.
Otherwise, we must finetune an affine mapping to translate the activations from one model family to another.
To interpret <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> using LIT, we must finetune <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, regardless of whether <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}\neq\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}\neq\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. We include details about our LIT reproduction in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Patchscopes and LIT Reproduction ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13316v1/verb_intro.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:466/36;" width="466" height="36" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Two ways of verbalizing descriptions of model activations. In (a), <span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and <span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">SelfIE</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>)</cite> both patch the last token representation from target model <math id="S2.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into the interpretation prompt and use <math id="S2.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to verbalize this activation.
In (b), LIT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> patches an activation matrix from a layer (<math id="S2.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> tokens) of <math id="S2.F2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.F2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Choosing an interpretation prompt.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each verbalized activation requires an interpretation prompt <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and since verbalizers are LLMs, the choice of prompt can heavily influence the verbalized output <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ceballos-Arroyo et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            See Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> for additional analysis on prompt choice in verbalization.</span></span></span>
LIT is trained on Question Answering (QA), so <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is generally a question about the knowledge encoded in the activation vector.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            Though other tasks have been used, e.g., by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al. (2024)</a></cite>, we focus on QA-style prompts.</span></span></span>
On the other hand, because <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> requires no training, <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen flexibly depending on the task.
To inspect an activation, one can write <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a question (“What is the name of the city?”) or as a cloze-style completion (“The name of the city is <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:7.1pt;height:0.4pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>”).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verbalization settings.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The verbalization settings for <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> and LIT vary with respect to the activation patching and evaluation. We adopt the default hyperparmeters and settings from prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. To inspect <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> with LIT, we patch all activations from the given source layer into the first layer of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain a single output.
For <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>, we patch a single token activation from the given source layer <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> into all layers of <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs. For evaluating LIT, the correctness is determined by a single output. However, in the <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> case, correctness is generally evaluated across all outputs, and so if the answer is in <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">any</em> of the <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> outputs, then the answer is considered correct.<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            For more on <span id="footnote4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al. (2024)</a></cite> and Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Patchscopes and LIT Reproduction ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Evaluation is task specific, but patching a single source activation into all layers of the model is typically the evaluative approach.</span></span></span> For all experiments and methods, we compute the average across source layers <math id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> = 1 - 15 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            In early experiments, we tested using <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">all</span> source layers of <span id="footnote5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B</span> and found that performance was worse; to ensure efficiency with compute usage, we stay consistent with prior work and use source layers 1-15.</span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information?</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Does the verbalizer even need target model activations, or can it answer <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> using the original <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math> alone? If verbalizers can solve popular benchmarks without access to rich activation information, these evaluations will join a long history of datasets discarded because models performed well without using key information <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Goyal et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaushik &amp; Lipton, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">McCoy et al., 2019</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We show that activation descriptions only convey information that the verbalizer can obtain from the target model’s input directly.
Specifically, rather than encoding <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{i}^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{i}^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we directly prompt <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> with only <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
If these evaluations require privileged insights into <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s performance will suffer without access to <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s activations, as in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.
Otherwise, the verbalizer is producing plausible explanations unfaithful to <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use two models: <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dubey et al., 2024</a>)</cite><span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/meta-llama/Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct</a></span></span></span> and <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_serif">7</span></span>
                
                
                
              <a href="https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Ministral-8B-Instruct-2410" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url">https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Ministral-8B-Instruct-2410</a></span></span></span></span>. Both are the target model and verbalizer, so <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span> has been studied in prior verbalization research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and we investigate a different model, <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span>, alongside <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>.
We use <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> and LIT as our verbalization approaches and we finetune LIT on <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> for this (training details in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Patchscopes and LIT Reproduction ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).
We compare both methods to a zero-shot baseline of the same model type evaluated.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Across six factual feature extraction tasks, we reproduce scores for LIT (multiple activation approach) and <span id="S3.T1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> (single activation approach) on <span id="S3.T1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span> and <span id="S3.T1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>, using source layers <math id="S3.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=1-15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=1-15</annotation></semantics></math> for both models. Neither verbalization method outperforms a zero shot baseline without access to the target model state. <span id="S3.T1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> denotes the highest score for each model category. We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="S3.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferroni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
<table id="S3.T1.10" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S3.T1.10.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:48.4pt;">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left">Method</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">country_curr</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.4.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.4.1.1" class="ltx_p">food_country</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.5.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top">
<span id="S3.T1.10.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_p">ath_pos</span>
</span></th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">ath_sport</th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">prod_comp</th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">star_const</th>
<th id="S3.T1.10.1.9" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Average</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
