<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Evaluation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use feature extraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2024</a>)</cite> as our evaluation task, using six categories considered in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.
Each sample is a <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(subject, relation, object)</span> triple, e.g.,  <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(United States, currency, dollar)</span>.
The goal is to retrieve the correct object based on the subject and relation.
For dataset details, see Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Feature Extraction Dataset Information ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
We follow prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and generate <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≤</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq</annotation></semantics></math>20 tokens for each output;
if the answer appears anywhere in this output (ignoring case), it is considered correct.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="110.06" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 110.06" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,110.06) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 104.74 C 0 107.68 2.38 110.06 5.32 110.06 L 544.68 110.06 C 547.62 110.06 550 107.68 550 104.74 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFFF;" fill="#E6FFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 87.12 L 548.62 87.12 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 95.13)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Key Finding 1</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 15.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:4.29em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="62.11" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 59.42)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For the feature extraction tasks used in prior works, a zero-shot baseline achieves high performance (matching or surpassing results from prior verbalization methods) despite operating on <em id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">only</em> text inputs.
This implies that no privileged knowledge is necessary for these tasks.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Inverting Activations</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We next test whether it is possible to reconstruct <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s activations. If so, verbalizers can base responses on reconstructed inputs, establishing a viable alternative hypothesis to the assumption that verbalization conveys information beyond the input.<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
            
            
            
          One might argue that the entire point of prior work was to establish the extent to which activations encode input prompts, but this objective is better served by aiming at direct reconstruction—as we attempt next—than at arbitrary QA tasks around inputs.</span></span></span> If the verbalizer is mostly communicating information about the input text, it may not be valuable as an interpretability tool.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our goal is to <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">invert</span> <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s internal representations and recover the input text that induced them.
We outline the approach in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Using a trained inversion model to recover text inputs (<math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math>), we then answer prompts (<math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) using only the reconstructed text (<math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and an instruction-tuned model (not conditioned on activations).
Inversion is performed using an LLM <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, finetuned to reconstruct inputs from activations.
These reconstructed inputs <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> are then passed to the instruction-tuned model.
If the instruction-tuned model can successfully answer <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> using <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, then the activations must encode the text input with sufficient fidelity for the verbalizer to answer questions from information about the input alone.
If so, verbalization may not convey information about the internal processes of <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> beyond the encoded input text. With the approach detailed below, the inverter reconstructs inputs with high fidelity if it is given all tokens, as shown in Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Inversion Training Details And Outputs ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.13316v1/inversion_interpretation.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:134/29;" width="134" height="29" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span>We use the following setup to assess whether verbalization techniques communicate privileged information, or if they merely describe input texts. (a) An activation from target model <math id="S4.F3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is directly inverted with <math id="S4.F3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a separate model trained to do this. (b) We pass this (possibly imperfect) reconstruction <math id="S4.F3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.F3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S4.F3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to make a prediction, without access to <math id="S4.F3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> activations. Finally, (c) we obtain the output from <math id="S4.F3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a zeroshot judgment of the inverted input and the prompt, combined.
Note that <math id="S4.F3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is in this case an instruction-tuned model not trained on activations (though here, when paired with <math id="S4.F3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the notation interchangeably).</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the same verbalization setup from Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, where <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}=</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span>. For inversion, we use <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> = <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span> and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5-Base</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite>.
Encoder-decoder models like <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5</span> have been shown to be better inverters of single embeddings than decoder-only models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al., 2023</a>)</cite>.
We invert a single token activation with <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5-Base</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite> and <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span>.
To invert multiple activations, we only use <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span>. In Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 Ministral Results ‣ Appendix C Inversion Training Details And Outputs ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>, we use <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span> to invert <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span> activations in place of <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3</span>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Inversion and evaluation datasets.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al. (2023)</a></cite>, we train the inversion model on 8.8M unique passages from <span id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MS MARCO</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bajaj et al., 2018</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
              
              
              
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/Tevatron/msmarco-passage-corpus" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://huggingface.co/datasets/Tevatron/msmarco-passage-corpus</a></span></span></span> For evaluation, as in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we use feature extraction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, which <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> was not trained on. Prior work showed that inversion on out-of-domain datasets degrades performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, so our reconstructions may differ from the true inputs.
We use our trained <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> to reconstruct <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math> from the feature extraction dataset into <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, we predict without activations over <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
We again consider an output from any model correct if any its first 20 generated tokens contain the answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Evaluation on reconstructed inputs</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, we evaluate whether models can answer prompts on the basis of these reconstructions.<span id="footnote10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">10</span>
              
              
              
            If reconstructions contain extraneous information reflecting model internals, such privileged information might still be used by the modified verbalizer. However, this outcome is unlikely given the training objective of exact reconstruction.</span></span></span>
To do so, we train <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-Instruct</span> on <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> with the concatenated input sequences <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}+x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}+x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>—similar to our previous verbalization setup, but <em id="S4.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">without</em> activations. (Results using <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span> are in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 Ministral Results ‣ Appendix C Inversion Training Details And Outputs ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>.) We then use the model finetuned on <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> to answer feature extraction prompts given reconstructed inputs (so <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{rec}}+x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{rec}}+x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>).
We also compare to an additional <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span> model not trained on <span id="S4.SS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> as a zero-shot baseline. We present full experimental results in Tables <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Interpretation results. ‣ 4.1 Evaluation on reconstructed inputs ‣ 4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> and <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation results.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In both the single-activation (token-level, Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>) and multiple-activation (layer-based, Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Interpretation results. ‣ 4.1 Evaluation on reconstructed inputs ‣ 4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) settings, the verbalizer model usually extracts the correct answer from the reconstructed text. With layer-based inversion, it is possible to reconstruct and predict almost perfectly over each task, matching the verbalization baseline. Performance varies more using token-based inversion, but on half the tasks considered, we see the same performance as in the canonical setup.
We also compare both inversion approaches to the verbalization of a single token or layer (<math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell</annotation></semantics></math> = 15), patched into the inference pass of the verbalizer; since we only invert a single token or layer, the comparison is fairer. Under these conditions, inversion always outperforms activation verbalization.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Note that the evaluation conditions actually place our inversion-based approach at a disadvantage relative to verbalization. Because our zero-shot descriptions are generated by instruction-tuned models, their stereotypical verbosity may fail to answer the prompt within 20 tokens. By contrast, verbalizers are able to bypass the verbosity (directly or via training), giving them an edge over zero-shot interpretations. If our interpretation models were tuned for brevity or given more output tokens, they might perform even better on benchmarks relative to the verbalizers.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="93.45" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 93.45" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,93.45) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 88.13 C 0 91.07 2.38 93.45 5.32 93.45 L 544.68 93.45 C 547.62 93.45 550 91.07 550 88.13 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFFF;" fill="#E6FFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 70.51 L 548.62 70.51 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 78.53)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Key Finding 2</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 15.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:3.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="45.51" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 42.82)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Verbalization methods have high accuracy when they rely on decoded information about the input text reconstructed from target model activations, suggesting they may not convey privileged knowledge.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S4.T2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inversion then interpretation</span> on <span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multiple</span> activations (i.e. a layer)—similar to the approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2024)</a></cite>—yields performance comparable to (and often better than) LIT (averaged across <math id="S4.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=</annotation></semantics></math> 1 - 15).
For computational efficiency since a new inverter cannot be trained for each layer, we only invert activations at the layer <math id="S4.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math> and compare this to the average of their approach in LIT.
“Zero-shot” denotes a pre-trained instruction-tuned model, same type as <math id="S4.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, given only reconstructed input.
We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="S4.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the LIT baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferroni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="S4.T2.6.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:43.4pt;">
<span id="S4.T2.6.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">count_curr</th>
<th id="S4.T2.6.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">food_count</th>
