</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These findings show that the verbalizer’s responses to many evaluation examples could reflect the decoded <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{input}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>input</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{input}}</annotation></semantics></math>, despite its imperfect reconstruction.
In the case of LIT, performance can be reproduced <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solely</em> from the encoded input text, whereas for <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>, the performance can be mostly mimicked.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model?</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have shown in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> that verbalization may not communicate anything beyond the information in input text, at least as evaluated on the feature extraction task used in prior work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.<span id="footnote11" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">11</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">11</span>
            
            
            
          If so, the feature extraction task used in prior work may be a poor choice for evaluating verbalization strategies, at least if we are interested in such approaches describing privileged information encoded in internals.</span></span></span>
In this section we go further, showing that the verbalizer <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cannot</span> answer prompts correctly unless it can answer them from the input text alone.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments test verbalizers using prompts that require world knowledge; ideally <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> would tell us about the world knowledge of the <em id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target</em> model <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
But <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself an LLM with world knowledge of its own, so it can answer prompts without access to <math id="S5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> internals.
Therefore, verbalizers may respond to a prompt by drawing on <math id="S5.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s knowledge, by drawing on <math id="S5.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s knowledge, or by some combination of both.
To disentangle these possibilities, we consider multiple setups where <math id="S5.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is finetuned on a novel dataset, imbuing it with knowledge unknown to <math id="S5.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
These experiments will show that <math id="S5.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot answer prompts based on <math id="S5.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s knowledge through its activations.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><em id="S5.T3.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inversion then interpretation</em> on a <span id="S5.T3.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">single</span> token activation (similar to an approach in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al. 2024</a></cite>). We use <span id="S5.T3.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5-Base</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Raffel et al., 2020</a>)</cite> as our inversion model, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al. (2023)</a></cite>, along with <span id="S5.T3.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-Instruct</span>, and compare this to <span id="S5.T3.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> (averaged across <math id="S5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=</annotation></semantics></math> 1 - 15).
For computational efficiency since a new inverter cannot be trained for each layer, we only invert activations at the last token position in <math id="S5.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math> and compare this to the average of their approach in <span id="S5.T3.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>.
“Zero-shot” denotes a pre-trained instruction-tuned model, same type as <math id="S5.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, given only reconstructed input.
We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="S5.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the <span id="S5.T3.15" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferroni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T3.16" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
