<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span><span id="S5.SS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce a dataset called <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span>, which contains attributes and texts of fake individuals.
Because these individuals do not exist, it is unlikely that a model would encode their (fabricated) biographical information unless explicitly trained on this data.<span id="footnote12" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">12</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">12</span>
              
              
              
            <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Allen-Zhu &amp; Li (2024)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zucchet et al. (2025)</a></cite> use similar synthetic persona datasets.</span></span></span>
The datasets provide a testbed to examine whether the attributes of a persona that have been learned by <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> can be decoded from <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s activations using <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Because such knowledge should be unknown to <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by construction, a verbalizer can only correctly answer prompts about the dataset by faithfully communicating privileged information stored in <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s activations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Datasets.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider three experimental settings, all using variants of <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span>. The first dataset, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa}}</annotation></semantics></math>), is a dataset containing attributes that are sociodemographically correlated with the persona name. The second dataset, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Shuffled</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_shuffled</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}</annotation></semantics></math>), shuffles the attributes in <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span> to remove the sociodemographic correlations. Finally, the third dataset, <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Fantasy</span> (<math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_fantasy</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}</annotation></semantics></math>), contains personas and attributes that are not real. Details of the datasets and their curation are described in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 PersonaQA Dataset Details ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>. We curate these in different ways to evaluate when knowledge from <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is verbalized by <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For all datasets, we consider six attributes per persona: country of origin, favorite food, favorite sport, favorite drink, favorite music genre, and favorite board game.
We provide examples of these datasets (both the persona and text for training) in Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>. Based on the attributes, we generate biographies and interviews to train <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> on, with text details found in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Biography and interview details ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experimental setting.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We aim to investigate if <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> can verbalize knowledge only available in the activations of <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each dataset, we finetune a target model <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (from a base <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.1-8B</span> or <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>) on the biographies and interviews of the generated personas, so <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> learns factual information about them.
In Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ E.5 Verifying PersonaQA-Fantasy Knowledge in ℳ_1 ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>, we confirm that <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> internalizes <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_fantasy</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}</annotation></semantics></math> specifically, while a base model is unable to predict the fabricated characteristics. We provide more details about finetuning <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS7" title="E.7 PersonaQA Training ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.7</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use the same experimental setup from Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, using the existing verbalizers (untrained in the <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> approach, and trained on <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> for LIT).
For completeness, we also report results using our inversion approach on <span id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span> and variations in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS8" title="E.8 PersonaQA Inversion Results ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.8</span></a>.
We generate a set of out of domain (with respect to training datasets) questions that can be asked about personas and use them to induce activations <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
For each question, we generate up to 20 tokens and determine if the correct answer is among them, following prior experiments.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Performance on the <span id="S5.T4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span> datasets is measured with absolute accuracy (based on the existing evaluation) across six attributes (column titles). <math id="S5.T4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}=\mathcal{M}_{2}=</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S5.T4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama</span> family of models. We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="S5.T4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the zero-shot baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferroni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
