<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Results and takeaways</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present results across <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span> datasets in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Experimental setting. ‣ 5.1 PersonaQA ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>, and include supplemental results for <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span> in Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ E.10 PersonaQA Results on Ministral ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>. We include an additional comparison to assess the degree to which <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is relying on its own world knowledge (rather than reading off <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). The setting, shown in Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ E.9 PersonaQA Swapped Results ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> for only <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>, evaluates <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> responses conditioned on <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_shuffled</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}</annotation></semantics></math> activations, respectively, against both the shuffled and original target labels.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zero-shot prompting and verbalization achieve good performance based purely on the associations from <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA</span>, despite having no prior knowledge of the personas.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Experimental setting. ‣ 5.1 PersonaQA ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows that zero-shot, LIT, and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> achieves nonzero accuracy across the tasks.
LIT likely fares comparatively well because it was finetuned to answer questions succinctly.
Zero-shot prompting tends to yield lengthier outputs, which degrades performance as evaluated.
Naively, one might interpret the verbalization results as telling us about <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, but the zero-shot results confirm that nontrivial performance is achievable based on crude statistical associations between personas and attributes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verbalizers (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) rely too much on their own world knowledge to make predictions, even when it conflicts with the knowledge in the interpreted activations.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Because performance of <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> and LIT on <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Shuffled</span> is low in Table <a href="#S5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Experimental setting. ‣ 5.1 PersonaQA ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, it is likely that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is generating responses based on its own knowledge, rather than information from <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_shuffled</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Auxiliary results in Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T21" title="Table 21 ‣ E.9 PersonaQA Swapped Results ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> show that <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, when conditioned on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_shuffled</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_shuffled}}</annotation></semantics></math> activations, performs better on the <em id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">original</em> (unshuffled) labels than with the shuffled labels.
In other words: <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> does a better job of answering questions about its own internal knowledge than about what <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> knows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Verbalization fails when the knowledge from <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is misaligned.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neither <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> nor LIT exceed zero accuracy on <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn><mtext>pqa_fantasy</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}^{\text{pqa\_fantasy}}</annotation></semantics></math>, confirming that verbalizers are limited to their own world knowledge.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.3 </span>Training <math id="S5.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S5.SS3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>’s World Knowledge</h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, we evaluate whether finetuning <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on the same <span id="S5.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Fantasy</span> knowledge improves its verbalization accuracy.
If <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> must possess the same world knowledge as <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, its verbalization cannot faithfully describe activations beyond <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>’s knowledge.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we focus our additional experiments on <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>. We finetune our verbalizer in two steps. First, we finetune a <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> on a modified version of <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Fantasy</span> with more personas, via next token prediction over the biographies and interviews (similar to how <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> was trained in the prior section). Then, we continue to finetune using LIT on <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">LatentQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> to learn to verbalize activations, same as the verbalization setup in Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. We also introduce a simple baseline used in existing work, which is a logistic probe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belinkov &amp; Glass, 2019</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belinkov, 2022</a>)</cite>.
A simple probe tests whether the representations from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> are extractable with minimal finetuning, without needing existing world knowledge.
If a probe performs better than a verbalizer, requiring world knowledge to properly verbalize with an LLM creates significant drawbacks. Finally, we also include the standard (untrained) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> approach. We use the same evaluation as prior for the verbalizers. For the probe, the evaluation selects the label with the highest probability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Dataset.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In our modified <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PersonaQA-Fantasy</span> dataset, we also include fewer labels for each attribute (up to 10) and more personas (200) compared to in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. We do so to generate enough samples for train and test splits such that the probe can be adequately trained, resulting in a train/test split of 160/40 unique personas, providing approximately 2600/600 train/test samples for finetuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Full table results are in Appendix Table <a href="#A5.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ E.11 Probing vs. Verbalization ‣ Appendix E PersonaQA Details and Results ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a>. Briefly, in both the (untrained) <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> setting and (trained) LIT setting, both achieve an accuracy of zero across all attribute datasets.
Given the observations earlier in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the activations might not contain enough information to adequately represent each persona from <math id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.
However, the activations do indeed encode this information: the linear probe achieves an average performance of 25% across all tasks, which is higher than random chance at 10%.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="144.04" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 144.04" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,144.04) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 138.72 C 0 141.65 2.38 144.04 5.32 144.04 L 544.68 144.04 C 547.62 144.04 550 141.65 550 138.72 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFFF;" fill="#E6FFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 121.1 L 548.62 121.1 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 129.11)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Key Finding 3</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 16.65)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:6.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="96.09" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 92.63)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Verbalizer LLMs may be <span id="S5.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p2.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">too</span> reliant on their own parametric knowledge: They often verbalize their own knowledge rather than the information encoded in the target LLM’s activation which they purport to describe.
In contrast, simple probes (with relatively few parameters) can more easily extract information about the target LLM.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Mechanistic interpretability</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Saphra &amp; Wiegreffe, 2024</a>)</cite> seeks to characterize the inner workings of LLMs.<span id="footnote13" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">13</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">13</span>
            
            
            
          We do not attempt a comprehensive review of mechanistic interpretability here, and instead point the reader to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rai et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mueller et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharkey et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bereska &amp; Gavves (2024)</a></cite>.</span></span></span>
This work is most relevant to prior efforts which have sought to map internal activations to tokens <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">nostalgebraist, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Belrose et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yom Din et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pal et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and in particular to techniques which map activations to <span id="S6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">natural language descriptions</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bills et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mousi et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ghandeharioun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, rather than probability distributions of vocabulary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">nostalgebraist, 2020</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another line of work related to our contributions here asks whether models can truly introspect privileged information, with mixed findings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kadavath et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Binder et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>.
The present effort is novel as our focus is on verbalization methods that access activations to investigate whether these activations offer such privileged information.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, recent efforts have investigated the <em id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faithfulness</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jacovi &amp; Goldberg, 2020</a>)</cite> of natural language explanations with respect to model behaviors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atanasova et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hase et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, specifically for explanations like Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanham et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and individual neurons <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bills et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Singh et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Antverg &amp; Belinkov, 2022</a>)</cite>.
These works have shown that such explanations may provide unreliable descriptions of LLM behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Antverg &amp; Belinkov, 2022</a>)</cite>.
Our work offers an analogous analysis of explanations via verbalization techniques.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Conclusions</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There has been nascent interest in <em id="S7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">verbalization</em>, i.e., generating interpretable natural language descriptions of the internal activations of a target model.
Our findings highlight some open questions about such techniques, their evaluation, and the information that they in fact convey.
For example, using the feature extraction task, we showed that access to internals of the target model for verbalization is unnecessary to achieve adequate performance.
Thus, some existing evaluations for verbalization may only be useful as a diagnostic task for the existence of input information that is contained in the activation, rather than privileged information that is added from the target model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Further, in controlled experiments we found that generated descriptions may reflect the parametric knowledge of the LLM used for verbalization more than the internal knowledge of the target LLM.
Controlled evaluations reveal that verbalizers fail to access privileged knowledge when the knowledge only exists in the target model and not the verbalizer itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In sum, our findings show that existing verbalization approaches may be limited in terms of decoding privileged information from model activations.
The intuitive appeal of this general approach remains.
But our findings highlight the need for rigorous baselines and controlled task settings to evaluate such techniques for interpretability purposes.</p>
</div>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSSx1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection">Acknowledgments</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSSx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The authors thank Eric Todd, Koyena Pal, Chantal Shaib, Arnab Sen Sharma, Jenny Liang, Mei, and David Bau for helpful conversations and suggestions. We also thank the DeltaAI system at the National Center for Supercomputing Applications through allocation CIS240895 from the Advanced Cyberinfrastructure Coordination Ecosystem: Services &amp; Support (ACCESS) program, which is supported by National Science Foundation grants #2138259, #2138286, #2138307, #2137603, and #2138296. ML is supported by an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship, and AMCA is supported by Colombia’s Minciencias and Fulbright 2021 program.</p>
</div>
</section>
