<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Inverting <math id="A3.SS3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To invert an activation matrix <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>h</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, we train <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}=</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-Instruct</span> using the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2015</a>)</cite> with <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\text{lr}}=2e^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lr</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\text{lr}}=2e^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math> and an effective batch size of 128.
We train with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> with four A100s, training for approximately one epoch (we cut training short because we find that the model learns to invert very quickly based on the validation loss, and that an entire epoch may take several days).
Specifically, the LoRA parameters are: rank <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> = 16, <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> = 32. LoRA adapters were applied to all attention projection layers (<span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">q_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">k_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">v_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o_proj</span>), MLP layers (<span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gate_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">down_proj</span>), <span id="A3.SS3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lm_head</span> with dropout = 0.05. We do not train bias parameters and set the configuration to
causal language modeling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.4 </span>Inverting <math id="A3.SS4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></h3>

<div id="A3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For inverting <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-Instruct</span>, we manually insert a special token for the activation into the forward pass of <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>rec</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{rec}}</annotation></semantics></math> and replace that token with the activation. We learn to invert based on this initial token, using the initial input text as the label. For hyperparameters, we use: <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{lr}=1e^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>lr</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{lr}=1e^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, a batch size of 512, along with the Adam optimizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma &amp; Ba, 2015</a>)</cite>, and choose <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>, with the activation chosen being the last token of any prompt.
We train with LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2022</a>)</cite> over two epochs with four GH200s. Specifically, the LoRA parameters are: rank <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>r</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r</annotation></semantics></math> = 16, <math id="A3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> = 32. LoRA adapters were applied to all attention projection layers (<span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">q_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">k_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">v_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">o_proj</span>), MLP layers (<span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gate_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">up_proj</span>, <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">down_proj</span>), <span id="A3.SS4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">lm_head</span> with dropout = 0.05. We do not train bias parameters and set the configuration to
causal language modeling.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For inverting <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{\ell}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{\ell}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with <span id="A3.SS4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5-Base</span>, use the code from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Morris et al. (2023)</a></cite> and modify it minimally to accommodate inverting activations. We mostly use the the default hyperparameters, but change a few.
Specifically, we use an effective batch size of 512, a learning rate of <math id="A3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1e^{-3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1e^{-3}</annotation></semantics></math>, and we train for up to two days (we found that training was slow and model failed to converge, though qualitatively outputs seemed reasonable at this point). Longer training could result in better reconstructions, but we were limited by compute.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.5 </span><span id="A3.SS5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span> Results</h3>

<div id="A3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We include results on using a different model, <span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>, to invert the activations of the same type. In this setting, to invert multiple activations, we use <span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>. To invert a single activation, we use <span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span> and <span id="A3.SS5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">T5-Base</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We find that the results for inversion over all activations (Appendix Table <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ C.5 Ministral Results ‣ Appendix C Inversion Training Details And Outputs ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and just a single activation for <span id="A3.SS5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span> similarly holds as it does for <span id="A3.SS5.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3</span>. It is relatively easy to invert the input text from the activations, even for a model, like <span id="A3.SS5.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span>, that is somewhat architecturally different. Note that in deciding which layer to invert, we follow and consistently use <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>, even though <span id="A3.SS5.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral</span> has 36 layers. So, although we choose <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>, because task-specific information may be located in the middle-most layers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, the most optimal performance across inversion and verbalization results may not be <math id="A3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span><span id="A3.T8.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Inversion then interpretation</span> on <span id="A3.T8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">multiple</span> activations (i.e. a layer), for <span id="A3.T8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>, which is the companion result to Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Interpretation results. ‣ 4.1 Evaluation on reconstructed inputs ‣ 4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. This yields performance comparable to (and often better than) LIT (averaged across <math id="A3.T8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=</annotation></semantics></math> 1 - 15).
For computational efficiency since a new inverter cannot be trained for each layer, we only invert activations at the layer <math id="A3.T8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math> and compare this to the average of their approach in LIT.
‘Zeroshot” denotes a pre-trained instruction-tuned model, same type as <math id="A3.T8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, given only reconstructed input.
We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="A3.T8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the LIT baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferoni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
<table id="A3.T8.8" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A3.T8.8.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A3.T8.8.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:43.4pt;">
<span id="A3.T8.8.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A3.T8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">count_curr</th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">food_count</th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">ath_pos</th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">ath_sport</th>
<th id="A3.T8.8.1.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">prod_comp</th>
