<figure id="A3.T9" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 9: </span><em id="A3.T9.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Inversion then interpretation</em> on a <span id="A3.T9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">single</span> token activation for <span id="A3.T9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Ministral-8B-Instruct</span>, which is the companion result to Table <a href="#S5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 5 Are Generated Descriptions Faithful to the Target Model? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.
For computational efficiency since a new inverter cannot be trained for each layer, we only invert activations at the last token position in <math id="A3.T9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math> and compare this to the average of their approach in <span id="A3.T9.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>.
“Zero-shot” denotes a pre-trained instruction-tuned model, same type as <math id="A3.T9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, given only reconstructed input.
We denote where results are statistically significantly worse (<math id="A3.T9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;</annotation></semantics></math>0.05) compared to the <span id="A3.T9.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> baseline—per McNemar’s test with Bonferroni correction across the same baseline—with an an asterisk (*).</figcaption>
