<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Verbalization Sensitivity</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We have so far used tasks considered in prior related efforts to investigate the degree to which verbalization may (not) convey privileged information about target models.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We now consider an additional stress test for such approaches, similar to existing work on chain-of-thought <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lanham et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lyu et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, asking: How does the specific choice of prompt (<math id="A6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>) influence the verbalizations generated by <math id="A6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>? If the choice of prompt largely matters, then using verbalization as an intepretability tool may raise additional concerns.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A6.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13316v1/appendix_averaged_charts_svg-tex.svg" id="A6.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/314;" width="548" height="314"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span>We show the effect of using an <math id="A6.F4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is semantically similar or adversarial. We average across all tasks and tested prompts for space; see Appendix Subsection <a href="#A6.SS4" title="F.4 Verbalization Prompts ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F.4</span></a> for the full prompt and task breakdown.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.1 </span>Setup</h3>

<div id="A6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We manipulate the feature extraction dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hernandez et al., 2024</a>)</cite> from Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Does Verbalization Convey Privileged Information? ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#S4" title="4 Inverting Activations ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.
A <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> to extract a country is in the form <math id="A6.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> = “The country of origin for <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">x</span>”.
We create varying sets of prompts with slight perturbations (see Appendix Table <a href="#A6.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ F.4 Verbalization Prompts ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>).
Prompts S.1-S.4 are prompts that are <span id="A6.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">semantically</span> similar to but innocuously modified from the original prompt. Prompts A.1 and A.2 are <em id="A6.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adversarially</em> manipulated.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We insert biasing labels and language in two ways: one less emphatic (“I think the answer is…”) and one more absolute (“it must be…”); both similarly motivated from the prior work in analyzing faithfulness in chain-of-thought <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Turpin et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arcuschin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> reasoning.
To generate the semantically similar prompts, we use <span id="A6.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Claude-4-Sonnet</span>, and we write by hand the two adversarial prompts.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We experiment with only a single layer of <span id="A6.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> and LIT, specifically <math id="A6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>. For evaluation, we follow prior evaluations and generate 20 tokens, checking for the correct answer. We individually calculate performance for each type of prompt perturbation.

<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.2 </span>Results</h3>

<div id="A6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="77.62" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 77.62" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,77.62) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.32 L 0 72.3 C 0 75.24 2.38 77.62 5.32 77.62 L 544.68 77.62 C 547.62 77.62 550 75.24 550 72.3 L 550 5.32 C 550 2.38 547.62 0 544.68 0 L 5.32 0 C 2.38 0 0 2.38 0 5.32 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFFF;" fill="#E6FFFF" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.38 5.32 L 1.38 54.68 L 548.62 54.68 L 548.62 5.32 C 548.62 3.15 546.85 1.38 544.68 1.38 L 5.32 1.38 C 3.15 1.38 1.38 3.15 1.38 5.32 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 62.69)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:0.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.3" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.61)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Key Finding</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.07 15.89)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.7em;--ltx-fo-height:1.95em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="29.67" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 26.98)" width="507.82"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.7em;">
<span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.SS2.p1.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Verbalization, like prompting generally, is (overly) sensitive to phrasings. This further complicates interpretation of verbalizer outputs.</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our key finding is shown above. In Appendix Figure <a href="#A6.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we average four sensitivity prompts and two adversarial prompts, which are all randomly chosen. An overall trend shows: even among semantically similar prompts, we find a net negative loss in performance of -0.044 for LIT and -0.014 for <span id="A6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>. Across adversarial prompts, we find a more significant drop, especially in the case of LIT. While the delta for <span id="A6.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span> is much lower, we are only considering <math id="A6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>, which means that with more layers considered, there could be higher variance in performance. Full results on the experiments are found in the next subsection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.3 </span>Full Verbalization Results</h3>

<figure id="A6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13316v1/appendix_4_prompts_sensitivity_svg-tex.svg" id="A6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/364;" width="548" height="364"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>We show the effects of small prompt manipulations. For both LIT and <span id="A6.F5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>, we verbalize <math id="A6.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>. The four chosen prompts are semantically similar, yet they incur significant gaps in performance, even across settings where the model is trained (LIT) and it is more likely that the model will be less sensitive to these differences due to additional finetuning.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A6.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.13316v1/appendix_2_prompts_distractor_svg-tex.svg" id="A6.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/364;" width="548" height="364"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span>We show the significant effect of adding prompt distractors, with incorrect labels, to <math id="A6.F6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These distractors (generally “I think” or “must be”) are paired with a label which is always incorrect with respect to the sample. For both LIT and <span id="A6.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Patchscopes</span>, we verbalize <math id="A6.F6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell=15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell=15</annotation></semantics></math>. Refer to Appendix Table <a href="#A6.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ F.4 Verbalization Prompts ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> for the specific prompt format.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present full verbalization results, for each task individually.
Appendix Figure <a href="#A6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ F.3 Full Verbalization Results ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> reports the performance achieved using semantically similar prompts.
Across four such prompts performance varies, e.g., with task <span id="A6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">food_from_country</span> dropping as much as 60% in performance (prompt S.1) from the original prompt with only slight variation.
This is perhaps unsurprising in light of prior findings regarding LLM prompt sensitivity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Webson &amp; Pavlick, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, but nonetheless complicates the use of such techniques for interpretability purposes.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Appendix Figure <a href="#A6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ F.3 Full Verbalization Results ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>, we report results under adversarial perturbations.
These show that it is relatively easy for <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> to flip judgment when verbalizing an adversarial <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In other words, the verbalization from <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> may skew towards the contents of <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{\text{prompt}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mtext>prompt</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{\text{prompt}}</annotation></semantics></math> itself, as opposed to the activations extracted from <math id="A6.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">F.4 </span>Verbalization Prompts</h3>

<figure id="A6.T24" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 24: </span>We present the prompts used in the perturbation experiments in Section <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>. Each prompt is numbered 1-6 and corresponds to the numbers in the legends of Appendix Figures <a href="#A6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ F.3 Full Verbalization Results ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A6.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ F.3 Full Verbalization Results ‣ Appendix F Verbalization Sensitivity ‣ Do Natural Language Descriptions of Model Activations Convey Privileged Information?" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</figcaption>
